#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(136.8pt, 82.28pt, "Bell-Inequality Violation for Continuous, Non-Projective Measurements ")
  #line-at(224.85pt, 108.78pt, "Shalender Singh*")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(307.88pt, 108.78pt, ", ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(313.38pt, 108.78pt, "Santosh Kumar ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(210.6pt, 121.55pt, "PolariQon Inc., Palo Alto, California, USA")
  #line-at(267.35pt, 134.05pt, "(December 2025)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(285.63pt, 172.05pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 198.83pt, "Many solid-state quantum platforms do not permit sharp, projective measurements but instead yield ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 211.33pt, "continuous voltage or field traces under weak, non-demolition readout. In such systems, standard Bell ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 224.08pt, "tests based on dichotomic projective measurements are not directly applicable, raising the question of ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 236.83pt, "how quantum nonlocality can be certified from continuous time-series data. Here we develop a general ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 249.33pt, "theoretical framework showing that Bell–CHSH inequality violation can be extracted from continuous, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 262.07pt, "non-projective measurements without assuming any specific collapse model or phase distribution. We ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 274.6pt, "show that sufficiently long continuous measurements of a single entangled pair sample its internal phase-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 287.35pt, "probability structure, enabling effective dichotomic observables to be constructed through phase-sensitive ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 300.1pt, "projections and coarse-graining. The resulting Bell correlator is governed by two experimentally ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 312.6pt, "accessible resources: intrinsic single-qubit phase spread and nonlocal phase locking between qubits. We ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 325.35pt, "benchmark the resulting estimator against conventional projective-measurement CHSH tests implemented ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 337.85pt, "via quantum-circuit simulations using Qiskit, finding quantitative agreement in the Bell-violating regime ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 350.6pt, "without parameter fitting. Classical deterministic correlations cannot violate the CHSH bound, whereas ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 363.37pt, "quantum phase-locked systems recover the nonlinear angular dependence characteristic of entanglement. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 375.87pt, "Our results provide a practical route to demonstrating Bell nonlocality in platforms where measurements ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 388.62pt, "are inherently continuous and weak. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 415.12pt, "1. INTRODUCTION ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 441.9pt, "Nonlocal quantum correlations, as formalized through Bell inequalities, remain among the most ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 454.4pt, "fundamental signatures distinguishing quantum mechanics from classical hidden-variable theories [1–3]. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 467.15pt, "Experimental demonstrations of Bell inequality violation have traditionally relied on projective, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 479.9pt, "dichotomic measurements performed on well-isolated two-level systems—most notably in photonic ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 492.4pt, "platforms where polarization analyzers naturally implement arbitrary measurement bases [4–7]. In ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 505.15pt, "contrast, many emerging solid-state quantum platforms—including superconducting circuits under ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 517.67pt, "continuous dispersive readout, spin ensembles, polariton condensates, and other driven–dissipative ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 530.42pt, "quantum materials—do not provide sharp, pulsed projective measurements. Instead, they yield continuous ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 543.17pt, "voltage or field traces whose structure reflects a combination of local phase dynamics, stochastic ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 555.67pt, "diffusion, and nonlocal correlations mediated by the underlying quantum field [8–13]. This departure ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 568.42pt, "from the traditional measurement paradigm raises a fundamental question: how can Bell nonlocality be ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 580.93pt, "identified and certified when the experimentally accessible observables are continuous time-series rather ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 593.7pt, "than discrete measurement outcomes? ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 620.45pt, "Prior theoretical approaches to Bell tests with continuous variables have focused on parity measurements, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 632.95pt, "phase-space quasiprobabilities, or homodyne-based inequalities [14–20]. While successful in optical ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 645.7pt, "settings, these approaches typically assume access to idealized quadrature measurements and do not ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 658.2pt, "directly apply to solid-state systems where measurement is weak, continuous, and intrinsically non-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 670.98pt, "projective. In such systems, the measurement does not collapse the quantum state into a discrete ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 683.72pt, "eigenbasis but instead produces a stochastic trajectory whose statistics encode partial information about ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 696.225pt, "the underlying quantum phase structure. Extracting Bell-type correlations from such trajectories therefore ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 708.975pt, "requires a framework that explicitly incorporates the statistical structure of continuous measurement ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 82.28pt, "records, rather than attempting to approximate projective outcomes. In this work, we provide such a ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 94.78pt, "framework and show that Bell–CHSH inequality violation can be recovered from continuous weak ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 107.53pt, "measurements by identifying the effective dichotomic observables naturally encoded in phase-resolved ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 120.3pt, "time-series data. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 146.8pt, "In this work, we address this gap by developing a theoretical framework that enables Bell–CHSH analysis ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 159.55pt, "to be carried out directly on continuous measurement records under explicit physical and statistical ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 172.05pt, "assumptions. Our approach constructs operational dichotomic observables from phase-resolved voltage ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 184.8pt, "traces, allowing continuous signals to be mapped onto a reduced measurement space compatible with the ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 197.33pt, "CHSH formalism. We show that, irrespective of microscopic details, the relevant measurement statistics ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 210.08pt, "collapse onto a two-dimensional angular subspace associated with second-harmonic phase dependence. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 222.83pt, "Within this subspace, Bell correlations are governed by two distinct and experimentally accessible ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 235.33pt, "resources: the local phase coherence of each subsystem, quantified by a distribution-dependent factor ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 248.33pt, "𝜅(𝑃)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(94.55pt, 248.33pt, ", and nonlocal phase locking between subsystems, quantified by a complex coherence parameter ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(521.47pt, 248.33pt, "𝛾")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(527.47pt, 248.33pt, ". ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 274.85pt, "Crucially, we derive a rigorous classical bound for the resulting CHSH estimator under a broad class of ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 287.6pt, "stationary local stochastic models consistent with continuous measurement and locality constraints. This ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 300.35pt, "allows us to identify a necessary and sufficient condition for Bell–CHSH violation expressed solely in ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 313.1pt, "terms of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(111.05pt, 313.1pt, "𝜅(𝑃)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(133.55pt, 313.1pt, "and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(152.33pt, 313.1pt, "𝛾")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.33pt, 313.1pt, ". By making explicit the physical assumptions underlying the measurement process ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 325.85pt, "and the associated classical null hypothesis, our framework provides a principled and transparent route to ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 338.35pt, "probing nonclassical correlations in systems where conventional projective Bell tests are impractical. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 351.1pt, "More broadly, it extends Bell-type analysis to a wide class of continuously monitored many-body ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 363.62pt, "quantum platforms, opening new avenues for both foundational studies and experimentally relevant tests ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 376.37pt, "of nonlocality beyond the projective measurement paradigm. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 407.12pt, "2. THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 440.15pt, "In this section we develop a general framework for evaluating Bell–CHSH correlations from continuous ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 452.65pt, "measurement records. Our starting point departs from the conventional Bell-test paradigm in which ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 465.4pt, "dichotomic outcomes arise from instantaneous projective measurements performed on repeated ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 477.9pt, "experimental realizations. Instead, we consider quantum systems accessed through continuous or weak ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 490.65pt, "measurement, where information about the state is encoded in time-resolved signals and must be ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 503.4pt, "extracted operationally from extended measurement records. The central question we address is therefore ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 515.92pt, "not how Bell inequalities emerge from idealized projective measurements, but under what physical and ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 528.67pt, "statistical assumptions Bell–CHSH structure can be meaningfully defined, bounded, and potentially ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 541.17pt, "violated when only continuous measurement data are available. We show that, despite the apparent high ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 553.92pt, "dimensionality of continuous records, Bell–CHSH correlations are governed by a reduced and universal ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 566.67pt, "measurement structure. By making explicit the assumptions underlying the measurement process and the ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 579.17pt, "associated classical null hypothesis, we establish a principled route from raw continuous signals to well-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 591.95pt, "defined Bell–CHSH correlators, enabling nonclassical correlations to be assessed in regimes where ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 604.7pt, "conventional projective Bell tests are inaccessible. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 635.2pt, "2.1 Phase-encoded two-qubit state and measurement operators")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 661.95pt, "We consider a two-qubit system accessed through continuous or weak measurement, as encountered in ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 674.47pt, "solid-state and driven–dissipative quantum platforms where experimentally available observables arise ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 687.22pt, "from time-resolved voltage or field signals rather than discrete projective outcomes. Throughout this ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 699.725pt, "work, we restrict attention to measurement regimes in which information about each subsystem is ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 712.475pt, "acquired gradually over time and can be characterized by stationary statistics. In this regime, time-")
]

