#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 82.28pt, "To assess the robustness of the framework beyond idealized analytic limits, we applied the full analysis ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 94.78pt, "pipeline of Sec. 2.6 to synthetic continuous-measurement records generated under controlled classical and ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 107.53pt, "quantum-consistent phase-locking models. The results are summarized in Fig. 1. Synthetic phase-resolved ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 120.05pt, "data were first processed through a conventional CHSH evaluation implemented using Qiskit quantum-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 132.8pt, "circuit simulations with projective, collapsible measurements. The same data were then analyzed using ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 145.8pt, "the reduced-phase estimator developed here, employing the predicted nonlinear correlator ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(470.2pt, 145.8pt, "𝐸(𝐴, 𝐵) =")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 158.8pt, "𝜅")
  #line-at(80.275pt, 158.8pt, "𝛾cos⁡[2(𝐴−𝐵)]")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(154.58pt, 158.8pt, "for a Gaussian phase-difference kernel, without any parameter fitting. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 185.3pt, "Above the CHSH violation threshold, the reduced-phase estimator tracks the Qiskit benchmark with high ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 198.08pt, "quantitative accuracy and crosses the classical bound ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.13pt, 198.08pt, "𝑆= 2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(334.65pt, 198.08pt, "at the same correlation scale ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(461.95pt, 198.08pt, "𝜎")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(467.7pt, 200.33pt, "𝐿")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(472.7pt, 198.08pt, ". Below the ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 210.83pt, "classical bound, the two approaches diverge, with the reduced-phase estimator exhibiting a more rapid ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 223.58pt, "decay. This behavior reflects the estimator’s tighter sensitivity to phase-locking coherence rather than a ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 236.08pt, "loss of validity. Importantly, the agreement in the violation regime—where entanglement is present and ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 248.83pt, "experimentally relevant—demonstrates that the proposed estimator reliably identifies both the presence ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 261.33pt, "and degree of entanglement between two qubits under continuous measurement. The sharper decay below ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 274.1pt, "the classical threshold further indicates that the estimator provides a conservative bound on nonclassical ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 286.85pt, "correlations, which is appropriate for entanglement detection. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 313.35pt, "The framework developed here establishes a principled extension of Bell–CHSH analysis to quantum ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 326.1pt, "systems accessed through continuous or weak measurement. Rather than relying on discrete projective ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 338.6pt, "outcomes accumulated over many experimental realizations, our approach defines Bell–CHSH structure ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 351.35pt, "directly at the level of time-resolved measurement records, under explicit physical and statistical ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 364.12pt, "assumptions. This shift is not merely technical: it clarifies how nonclassical correlations can be ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 376.62pt, "meaningfully assessed in platforms where projective measurements are either impractical or ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 389.37pt, "fundamentally incompatible with the underlying dynamics. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 415.87pt, "A central outcome of this work is the identification of two distinct and jointly necessary resources ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 428.62pt, "governing Bell–CHSH correlations in continuously monitored systems. The first is local phase coherence, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 441.65pt, "quantified by the distribution-dependent factor ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(280.13pt, 441.65pt, "𝜅(𝑃)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.63pt, 441.65pt, ", which captures intrinsic phase uncertainty at the ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 454.15pt, "level of individual subsystems as sampled by the measurement process. The second is nonlocal phase ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 467.15pt, "locking, quantified by the complex parameter ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.13pt, 467.15pt, "𝛾")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(281.13pt, 467.15pt, ", which encodes coherent correlations between ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 479.65pt, "subsystems. Bell–CHSH violation requires both resources: the absence of either reduces the correlations ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 492.4pt, "to those attainable by stationary local stochastic models processed through the same estimator. This ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 504.9pt, "decomposition provides a transparent physical interpretation of Bell violations in continuous-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 517.67pt, "measurement settings and highlights the fact that nonlocality in such systems is not a monolithic property ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 530.42pt, "but arises from the interplay of local and nonlocal coherence. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 556.92pt, "An important conceptual aspect of the present framework is the emergence of a reduced and universal ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 569.67pt, "measurement structure. Despite the apparent high dimensionality of continuous phase-resolved records, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 582.17pt, "Bell–CHSH correlations depend only on the second angular harmonic of the measured phase statistics, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 594.95pt, "effectively collapsing the problem onto a two-dimensional subspace compatible with the CHSH ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 607.7pt, "formalism. This reduction is independent of microscopic details and does not rely on a particular choice ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 620.2pt, "of phase-locking kernel, as illustrated by the analytically tractable Gaussian example. The resulting ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 632.95pt, "universality suggests that Bell–CHSH structure in continuous measurement is far more constrained than ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 645.7pt, "might be expected from the richness of the underlying dynamics. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 672.22pt, "The framework is explicitly assumption-dependent and does not aim to provide a loophole-free or device-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 684.97pt, "independent Bell test. Instead, it establishes a rigorous classical benchmark for a well-defined class of ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 697.475pt, "continuous-record estimators under locality and stationarity constraints. Within this regime, violations of ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 710.225pt, "the Bell–CHSH bound unambiguously signal correlations that cannot be reproduced by local classical ")
]

// arXiv source page 20
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.025pt, 82.28pt, "stochastic processes subjected to the same measurement and analysis pipeline. This perspective aligns ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 94.78pt, "naturally with experimental practice in solid-state and driven–dissipative platforms, where continuous ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 107.53pt, "monitoring and fixed signal-processing protocols are the norm. The synthetic benchmarks shown in Figs. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 120.05pt, "1–3 explicitly illustrate this classical boundary by demonstrating that no violation arises when ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 132.8pt, "deterministic or locally stochastic signals are processed through the same estimator. ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 159.55pt, "Beyond its foundational implications, the present approach has direct relevance for a broad class of ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 172.05pt, "quantum platforms, including polariton condensates, superconducting circuits under continuous readout, ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 184.8pt, "and other mesoscopic many-body systems. In such settings, Bell–CHSH analysis has traditionally been ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 197.33pt, "regarded as inaccessible or ambiguous due to the absence of projective measurements. By providing a ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 210.08pt, "clear operational route from continuous measurement records to Bell–CHSH correlators with a well-")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 222.83pt, "defined classical bound, this work expands the experimental scope of Bell-type nonlocality and offers a ")
  #line-at(72.025pt, 235.58pt, "unifying language for comparing nonclassical correlations across disparate physical systems.")
]

